/* ============================================================
   Lesson 36 deep dive
   ============================================================ */
const DEEP36 = `
<div class="deep">
  <h3>Lesson 36 in detail — the three 〜ように patterns</h3>

  <div class="pat">
    <h4>1. 〜ように、〜</h4>
    <p class="gloss">“So that …” — states a goal you cannot directly control, followed by an action you take to reach it.</p>
    <div class="form">
      <b>[potential verb / intransitive verb / ない-form]　＋　ように、　＋　[deliberate action]</b><br>
      The verb before ように is never a plain volitional action. The two clauses may have different subjects.
    </div>
    <ul class="ex">
      <li><span class="j">{日本語|にほんご}が{話|はな}せる ように、{毎日|まいにち}{練習|れんしゅう}して います。</span>
        <span class="t">So that I can speak Japanese, I practise every day.</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">よく{見|み}える ように、{電気|でんき}を つけました。</span>
        <span class="t">I turned the light on so it would be easy to see.</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">{忘|わす}れない ように、メモを して おきます。</span>
        <span class="t">I take notes in advance so that I won't forget.</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">{風邪|かぜ}が{早|はや}く{治|なお}る ように、{薬|くすり}を{飲|の}みました。</span>
        <span class="t">I took medicine so that my cold would get better quickly.</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">{子|こ}どもが{読|よ}める ように、ひらがなで{書|か}きました。</span>
        <span class="t">I wrote it in hiragana so that children can read it. (different subjects)</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><b>Watch for:</b> the three verb types that fit before ように are potential
      (<span class="jp">{話|はな}せる</span>), intransitive
      (<span class="jp">{治|なお}る、{見|み}える、{聞|き}こえる</span>) and negative
      (<span class="jp">{忘|わす}れない</span>). If you catch yourself putting a plain volitional verb
      there, you almost certainly want ために instead.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="pat">
    <h4>2. 〜ように なります</h4>
    <p class="gloss">“Came to be able to …” / “started …ing” — a gradual change in ability, habit or situation.</p>
    <div class="form">
      <b>[dictionary form / potential form]　＋　ように なりました</b> — for a change that has happened.<br>
      <b>[ない-form]　＋　ように なりました</b> — for a habit that stopped (but see the note below).
    </div>
    <ul class="ex">
      <li><span class="j">{毎日|まいにち}{練習|れんしゅう}したら、{泳|およ}げる ように なりました。</span>
        <span class="t">After practising every day, I became able to swim.</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">{子|こ}どもは{野菜|やさい}を{食|た}べる ように なりました。</span>
        <span class="t">My child has started eating vegetables.</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">{日本|にほん}に{住|す}んでから、{魚|さかな}が{好|す}きに なりました。</span>
        <span class="t">Since living in Japan I've come to like fish. (adjective → 〜に なる, not ように)</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">{最近|さいきん}、テレビを{見|み}なく なりました。</span>
        <span class="t">Lately I've stopped watching TV.</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><b>The 〜なく なりました shortcut:</b> for losing an ability or dropping a habit, Japanese
      strongly prefers <span class="jp">{泳|およ}げなく なりました</span> and
      <span class="jp">テレビを{見|み}なく なりました</span> over
      <span class="jp">〜ないように なりました</span>. The listening workbook treats this as its own pattern
      and gives it a dedicated task (CD B-8), right between 〜ようになりました and 〜ようにしています — so it's
      expected to be drilled as a separate form, not as an afterthought.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="pat">
    <h4>3. 〜ように して います / 〜ように して ください</h4>
    <p class="gloss">“I make a point of …” / “Please make sure you …” — sustained effort rather than a single act.</p>
    <div class="form">
      <b>[dictionary form / ない-form]　＋　ように して います</b> — an effort you keep making.<br>
      <b>[dictionary form / ない-form]　＋　ように して ください</b> — asking for ongoing care, not a one-off action.
    </div>
    <ul class="ex">
      <li><span class="j">{毎朝|まいあさ}{運動|うんどう}する ように して います。</span>
        <span class="t">I make a point of exercising every morning.</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">{甘|あま}い ものを{食|た}べない ように して います。</span>
        <span class="t">I'm trying not to eat sweet things.</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">{約束|やくそく}の{時間|じかん}に{遅|おく}れない ように して ください。</span>
        <span class="t">Please make sure you're not late for appointments.</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">{野菜|やさい}を たくさん{食|た}べる ように して ください。</span>
        <span class="t">Please make a habit of eating plenty of vegetables.</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><b>して います vs して ください vs します:</b>
      <span class="jp">〜ように します</span> announces a resolution you're making now;
      <span class="jp">〜ように して います</span> reports an effort already underway;
      <span class="jp">〜ように して ください</span> asks for a lasting habit, where plain
      <span class="jp">〜て ください</span> would ask for one specific action.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="pat">
    <h4>ように vs ために — the distinction to drill</h4>
    <table class="cmp">
      <tr><th style="width:16%"></th><th style="width:42%">〜ように</th><th style="width:42%">〜ために (Lesson 42)</th></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Verb before it</b></td>
        <td>Potential, intransitive, or ない-form — outcomes you can't simply decide</td>
        <td>Volitional dictionary form — something you actively do</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Subjects</b></td>
        <td>May differ between the two clauses</td>
        <td>Must be the same in both clauses</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Example</b></td>
        <td class="j">{日本語|にほんご}が{上手|じょうず}に なる ように、{毎日|まいにち}{勉強|べんきょう}して います。</td>
        <td class="j">{日本語|にほんご}を{習|なら}う ために、{日本|にほん}へ{来|き}ました。</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Feel</b></td>
        <td>Hoping for a result</td>
        <td>Acting toward a goal</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="pat">
    <h4>Verbs worth having ready for this lesson</h4>
    <p class="gloss">These pair naturally with the 〜ように patterns, so they show up constantly in Lesson 36 practice.</p>
    <ul class="ex">
      <li><span class="j">{治|なお}ります・{治|なお}します</span><span class="t">get better (intransitive) / cure, fix</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">{届|とど}きます</span><span class="t">reach, be delivered</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">{通|かよ}います</span><span class="t">commute to, attend regularly</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">{続|つづ}けます・{続|つづ}きます</span><span class="t">continue (transitive / intransitive)</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">{取|と}れます・{見|み}えます・{聞|き}こえます</span><span class="t">come off / be visible / be audible</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">{慣|な}れます</span><span class="t">get used to (〜に{慣|な}れます)</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">{間|ま}に{合|あ}います</span><span class="t">be in time for</span></li>
      <li><span class="j">{頑張|がんば}ります</span><span class="t">do one's best, persevere</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><b>Note:</b> this is a working list of high-frequency verbs for these patterns, not the
      textbook's official Lesson 36 vocabulary page. Add the exact 語彙 list from your book (or from Nao san)
      in the notes field below so the two stay in sync.</p>
  </div>
</div>`;

