    explain: "This passes on information you got from somewhere else, exactly as you received it and without adding any judgement of your own. When you want to name where it came from, put ～に よると at the head of the sentence. Do not confuse it with the appearance そうです of Lesson 43: that one attaches to the ます-stem or to the bare adjective, so 雨が 降りそうです is 'it looks like rain', while the plain form here gives 雨が 降るそうです, 'I hear it will rain'. It also differs from ～と 言って いました of Lesson 33, which names the person who spoke; そうです leaves the source open unless you state it.",
    formation: "[plain form] ＋ そうです　（な-adj and N keep だ：きれいだそうです、休{やす}みだそうです）　／　［N に よると］、～そうです",
